  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        attributed-text-segment-list__content-truncated" dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">𝐖𝐞 𝐩𝐮𝐭 𝐚 𝐙𝐢𝐯𝐢𝐝 3 𝐜𝐚𝐦𝐞𝐫𝐚 𝐢𝐧 𝐚 75°𝐂 𝐬𝐚𝐮𝐧𝐚. 🔥🔥🔥

Loading docks in winter with the doors wide open, welding cells radiating heat... Temperature swings are everywhere in real production: it can result in 𝐜𝐚𝐥𝐢𝐛𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐝𝐫𝐢𝐟𝐭𝐬 and 𝐩𝐨𝐨𝐫 𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐟𝐨𝐫𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞 for your 3D camera.

The datasheet says the optimal temperature range for our Zivid cameras is 0-45°C. Our mad scientist from the Quality and Test team <a class="link" href="https://no.linkedin.com/in/erik-m%C3%B8renskog-9bba60181?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>Erik Mørenskog</a> wanted to show you exactly how they perform under extreme temperatures:

 ➡️ Capture 1: in a sauna at 75°C (yes, well past our rated 45°C!)
 ➡️ Capture 2: in the walk-in fridge of our canteen at 3°C

What did the point clouds look like and how was the calibration under these extreme conditions? Watch the video and find out 👇

Learn more about Zivid 3: <a class="link" href="https://lnkd.in/dqxwBmZy?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" rel="nofollow" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>https://lnkd.in/dqxwBmZy</a>

